In article <1990Oct9.075719.17274@zorch.SF-Bay.ORG> xanthian@zorch.SF-Bay.ORG (Kent Paul Dolan) writes:
>In article <106783@convex.convex.com> swarren@convex.com (Steve Warren) writes:
>>I think that the obvious solution is kicking.  Kicking your pet should not be
>>interpreted by the game as an attack against your pet, but rather an attempt
>>to motivate it to get out of your way.  Thus it should not do any hit-point
>>damage to your pet and it should not cause you to loose any luck or anger
>>dieties.
>
>This is fine right up until the nymphs steal your weapons, the gods have
>given up paying attention to your incessant whining, the cupboard is bare,
>you're having trouble staying on your feet, and Fido is beginning to look
>more and more like Phideaux-burger on the hoof.

Yes, this would be more difficult.  You would actually have to leave the
level and return before you could kick Fido to death.

Of course, once you start kicking him at that point he will be a decidedly
unfriendly animal, and your perverse advantage over him will be lost.

That would then become one of the natural results of allowing a nymph to
steal all your weapons and getting into the other troubles you mentioned.

If you don't want to be in such trouble, then don't get into such trouble
;^)  ;^)  ;^)  ;^)  ;^)  ;^)  ;^)  ;^)  ;^)  ;^)  ;^)  ;^)  ;^)  ;^)  ;^)

I am just remembering my dog and how I used to get out of the house when he
was standing between me and the door (obviously intent on escaping with me).
I would hook my foot under his ribs and with a kicking motion that didn't
actually hurt him, I would force him around out of my way.  Once I started
this action he would go along with it, moving behind me.  He was a medium
sized dog (an over-sized spitz).

